{"timestamp": "2026-09-07T16:00:24+08:00", "level": "INFO", "event": "request_started", "request_id": "req_128111", "model": "ilmu/ilmu-mini-v3.3", "user_message": "summary里面的内容\n\n--- [Document Content Extracted via Document Parser] ---\n--- [Page 1] ---\nThe Comprehensive Malaysian Context Knowledge\nBase for AI Systems\nThis document serves as the core knowledge base for AI agents operating within the Malaysian context. It covers\ntaxation, provident funds, government standard operating procedures (SOPs), dispute resolution, SME regulations,\nand local linguistic context.\nDomain 1: Taxation (LHDN) & Employees Provident Fund (KWSP/EPF)\n1.1 Individual Income Tax Reliefs (LHDN - Inland Revenue Board)\nIndividual & Dependent Relatives: RM9,000 base relief.\nMedical Expenses (Self, Spouse, Child): Up to RM10,000 for serious illnesses, fertility treatments,\nvaccinations (up to RM1,000), and complete medical examinations (up to RM1,000).\nLifestyle Relief: Up to RM2,500. Covers reading materials, sports equipment,\ncomputers/smartphones/tablets, and monthly internet subscriptions.\nAdditional Lifestyle Relief (Sports): Up to RM1,000 speciﬁcally for purchasing sports equipment, facility\nrentals, or tournament fees.\nLife Insurance & EPF: Up to RM7,000 (Non-public servants: RM3,000 insurance + RM4,000 EPF).\nEducation Fees: Up to RM7,000 for approved courses (Masters/PhD) or upskilling/reskilling courses (up to\nRM2,000).\nElectric Vehicle (EV) Charging Facilities: Up to RM2,500.\n1.2 Employees Provident Fund (EPF/KWSP)\nStatutory Contribution Rates: Employees contribute 11% of their monthly salary; Employers contribute\n12% (if salary > RM5,000) or 13% (if salary <= RM5,000).\nAccount Restructuring (Eﬀective 2024):\nAkaun Persaraan (Account 1): 75% of contributions. Strict retirement account (age 55/60\nwithdrawal).\nAkaun Sejahtera (Account 2): 15% of contributions. Used for housing, education, and speciﬁc\nmedical expenses.\nAkaun Fleksibel (Account 3): 10% of contributions. Can be withdrawn at any time for any\npurpose via the KWSP i-Akaun app. Minimum withdrawal is RM50.\n1.3 Sales and Service Tax (SST)\nCurrent Rate: Service tax rate is 8% (increased from 6% eﬀective March 2024).\nExceptions: Food & Beverage (F&B), telecommunication services, and parking remain at 6%.\nDomain 2: Government Aid & Welfare\n2.1 Sumbangan Tunai Rahmah (STR)\nThe STR is a targeted cash assistance program for the B40 and lower M40 groups.\nEligibility Thresholds:\nHouseholds (Married/Single Parents): Monthly income <= RM5,000.\nSenior Citizens (No Spouse): Monthly income <= RM5,000.\nSingles (Age 21-59): Monthly income <= RM2,500.\nPayout Ranges: Varies from RM100 (Singles) up to RM3,700 (Households with 5+ children).\nDomain 3: Core Government Departments (SOPs & Fees)\n\n--- [Page 2] ---\n3.1 Jabatan Pengangkutan Jalan (JPJ) - Road Transport Department\nDriving License Renewal (CDL): RM30 per year (Car/Class D) or RM20 per year (Motorcycle/Class B2).\nUsers can renew for up to 10 years, which oﬀers a 10% discount for a 10-year renewal.\nRoad Tax (LKM): Based on engine capacity (cc). EVs currently enjoy road tax exemptions, transitioning to a\nnew kilowatt-based formula soon.\nMyJPJ App: Digital driving licenses (e-LMM) and digital road taxes (e-LKM) are oﬃcially recognized. Physical\ncopies are no longer strictly required for private vehicles.\n3.2 Jabatan Imigresen Malaysia (Immigration)\nMalaysian International Passport: Valid for 5 years.\nFees: Normal rate is RM200. Senior citizens (age 60+), children under 12, and Hajj pilgrims pay RM100.\nOKU (Persons with Disabilities) are free.\nProcess: Online renewals are encouraged via the MyOnline Passport system.\n3.3 Jabatan Pendaftaran Negara (JPN) - National Registration\nLost MyKad Penalty: First loss (RM100), Second loss (RM300), Third and subsequent losses (RM1,000).\nExemptions apply if the loss is due to a crime (police report required) or natural disaster.\nDomain 4: Dispute Resolution & Rights\n4.1 Tribunal Tuntutan Pengguna Malaysia (TTPM) - Consumer Claims Tribunal\nPurpose: A fast, inexpensive alternative to civil courts for consumers to claim damages against businesses\n(e.g., defective goods, unfulﬁlled services, online shopping scams).\nClaim Limit: Up to RM50,000.\nFiling Fee: RM5.\nExclusions: Does not cover land/real estate disputes, personal injury, or issues already in civil court.\nLawyers are generally not allowed to represent parties; individuals must represent themselves.\n4.2 Jabatan Tenaga Kerja (JTK) - Labour Department\nPurpose: Resolving disputes between employees and employers (e.g., unpaid salaries, wrongful dismissal,\ndenial of maternity leave, unpaid overtime).\nCoverage: Since the 2023 Employment Act amendments, all employees regardless of salary are covered\n(previously capped at RM2,000/month). However, overtime (OT) pay provisions are only applicable to those\nearning RM4,000 or less.\nDomain 5: SME & Business Regulations (SSM & JAKIM)\n5.1 Suruhanjaya Syarikat Malaysia (SSM) - Companies Commission\nEnterprise Registration (Sole Proprietorship / Partnership):\nUsing personal name as per MyKad: RM30 / year.\nUsing a trade name (e.g., \"Maju Jaya Enterprise\"): RM60 / year.\nBranch registration: RM5 per branch / year.\nSdn Bhd (Private Limited Company): Governed under the Companies Act 2016. Requires a Company\nSecretary and annual auditing, unlike Enterprises. Minimum paid-up capital is technically RM1.\n5.2 Halal Certiﬁcation (JAKIM)\nConcept: Halal certiﬁcation is voluntary but highly sought after in the F&B industry. It requires strict\nadherence to Shariah law, hygiene, and ingredient sourcing.\nTwo Muslim Workers Rule: Establishments applying for Halal certiﬁcation usually must have at least two\nlocal Muslim workers in the kitchen/food preparation area.\n\n--- [Page 3] ---\nDomain 6: Local Linguistic Context & Socio-Economic Classiﬁcations\n6.1 Socio-Economic Tiers\nMalaysia divides its population into three main income groups for policy targeting:\nB40 (Bottom 40%): Low-income earners. The primary target for government subsidies and cash aid (STR).\nM40 (Middle 40%): The middle class. Often target for tax reliefs and speciﬁc aﬀordable housing schemes.\nT20 (Top 20%): High-income earners. Subject to higher tax brackets and exclusion from generalized\nsubsidies (e.g., targeted fuel subsidies).\n6.2 Essential Local Slang (Manglish / Bahasa Rojak) for AI Systems\nTapau / Bungkus: To take away food.\nBelanja: To treat someone (e.g., paying for their meal).\nSaman: A ﬁne or summons (usually related to traﬃc tickets from PDRM or JPJ).\nBojio: FOMO (Fear Of Missing Out); \"Why didn't you invite me?\".\nCuti: Holiday or leave (e.g., \"I took cuti today\" = I took annual leave).\nMC: Medical Certiﬁcate (e.g., \"I took MC today\" = I took sick leave).\nUndercon: Under construction (typically referring to newly launched housing projects).\nLAD (Liquidated Ascertained Damages): Compensation payable by a developer to a buyer for late\ndelivery of a property.\n6.3 Public Holidays Dynamics\nMalaysia observes both Federal Holidays (applicable nationwide) and State Holidays (applicable only in\nspeciﬁc states).\nExample: Thaipusam is a public holiday in Kuala Lumpur, Selangor, Johor, Penang, Perak, and Negeri\nSembilan, but is a normal working day in other states.\nExample: Nuzul Al-Quran is observed in KL, Selangor, Penang, Perak, Perlis, Pahang, Terengganu, and\nKelantan, but not in Johor, Melaka, Sabah, or Sarawak.\n--------------------------------------------------------\n请仔细阅读上述文档内容，回答用户的问题。", "has_image": false, "history_len": 10}
